Fix: closing a finished thread's handle wiped the caller's current fiber (freeze after the intro movies)

Applies to: rexglue-sdk nightly-20260921-923c1a59 (thirdparty/rexglue-sdk)
Apply with: git -C thirdparty/rexglue-sdk apply ../../patches/rexglue-sdk/0002-fiber-destroy-wrong-thread.patch
Status:     local patch, not upstream yet (worth reporting)

WHAT BROKE
  After the intro movies ended (or were skipped), Crash froze on a still
  "Loading" frame. The game's main thread sat at 100% CPU in a fault that
  repeated forever: swapcontext() storing registers into address 0x80, i.e.
  into nullptr->context_. The SDK's SIGSEGV handler (installed for guest
  memory tricks) returned without fixing anything, so the same store ran
  again and again. No crash, no log line, just a frozen picture.

WHY
  rex::thread::Fiber keeps "the fiber running on this host thread" in a
  thread_local, tls_current_. Every XThread owns a host "main fiber"
  (XThread::Execute calls Fiber::ConvertCurrentThread), and ~XThread()
  destroys it. But ~XThread() runs on whichever thread drops the LAST
  reference to the XThread. Here that's the game's main thread: when a movie
  finishes, it NtClose()s the handles of 4 helper threads that have already
  exited. Fiber::Destroy() on a thread fiber did `tls_current_ = nullptr`
  unconditionally, clearing the *main* thread's pointer, not the dead
  thread's. The main thread's next SwitchToFiber (its task scheduler does
  one every frame) then read from = nullptr and swapcontext() faulted.

  The Win32 backend has the same flaw in a different shape: it calls
  ::ConvertFiberToThread(), which also acts on the calling thread.

THE FIX (src/core/fiber_posix.cpp, src/core/fiber_win32.cpp)
  Fiber::Destroy() only clears tls_current_ (and, on Win32, only calls
  ::ConvertFiberToThread()) when this fiber is the one currently running on
  the calling thread. Destroying another thread's fiber is then just a
  delete, which is all it needs: that thread has exited, and a thread fiber
  owns no stack. The Win32 half is untested (we only build Linux so far).

HOW WE FOUND IT: docs/findings/05-post-intro-freeze-fibers.md

diff --git a/src/core/fiber_posix.cpp b/src/core/fiber_posix.cpp
index 4fe1ca6..f8f139c 100644
--- a/src/core/fiber_posix.cpp
+++ b/src/core/fiber_posix.cpp
@@ -69,11 +69,17 @@ void Fiber::SwitchTo(Fiber* target) {
 }
 
 void Fiber::Destroy() {
-  // Thread fibers are destroyed from the owning thread itself.
-  if (is_thread_fiber_) {
+  // tls_current_ belongs to the *calling* thread, which is not always the
+  // fiber's owner: ~XThread() destroys a thread's main fiber on whichever
+  // thread drops the last reference, typically one that NtClose()s the handle
+  // of a finished thread. Clearing the pointer unconditionally then wiped the
+  // closer's own current fiber, and its next SwitchTo() ran swapcontext() on
+  // nullptr->context_, a fault that retries forever (Crash: Mind over Mutant
+  // froze on "Loading" right after its intro movies). Only clear it when this
+  // fiber really is the one running on this thread.
+  if (tls_current_ == this) {
+    assert(is_thread_fiber_ && "Destroy called on the currently running fiber");
     tls_current_ = nullptr;
-  } else {
-    assert(this != tls_current_ && "Destroy called on the currently running fiber");
   }
   // No POSIX equivalent of ConvertFiberToThread; stack_ is freed by the vector destructor.
   delete this;
diff --git a/src/core/fiber_win32.cpp b/src/core/fiber_win32.cpp
index 5744a90..2bc679f 100644
--- a/src/core/fiber_win32.cpp
+++ b/src/core/fiber_win32.cpp
@@ -48,10 +48,16 @@ void Fiber::SwitchTo(Fiber* target) {
 }
 
 void Fiber::Destroy() {
-  // Thread fibers are destroyed from the owning thread itself.
+  // Thread fibers are usually destroyed by their owning thread, but ~XThread()
+  // can also run on another thread (the one that NtClose()s the last handle of
+  // a finished thread). ::ConvertFiberToThread() and tls_current_ both act on
+  // the *calling* thread, so only touch them when this fiber is the one
+  // running here; otherwise we'd tear down the closer's own fiber state.
   if (is_thread_fiber_) {
-    ::ConvertFiberToThread();
-    tls_current_ = nullptr;
+    if (tls_current_ == this) {
+      ::ConvertFiberToThread();
+      tls_current_ = nullptr;
+    }
   } else {
     assert(this != tls_current_ && "Destroy called on the currently running fiber");
     ::DeleteFiber(handle_);
